rtant for • Blood circulation • Blood pressure • Cellular function • Temperature regulation • Electrolyte balance --- **3 Dehydration** • Dehydration is a condition in which the body loses more water than it takes in resulting in insufficient body water • Causes • Vomiting • Diarrhea • Excessive sweating • Fever • Inadequate fluid intake • Blood/fluid loss • Clinical features • Thirst • Dry mouth • Reduced urine output • Dark urine • Dizziness • Weakness • Tachycardia • Hypotension in severe cases --- **4 Electrolyte Imbalance** • Electrolyte imbalance is an abnormal increase or decrease in the concentration of one or more electrolytes in body fluids • Common abnormalities • Hyponatremia / hypernatremia • Hypokalemia / hyperkalemia • Hypocalcemia / hypercalcemia • Hypomagnesemia / hypermagnesemia • Clinical features • Muscle weakness • Cramps • Fatigue • Confusion • Nausea/vomiting • Abnormal heart rhythm particularly with significant potassium abnormalities --- **5 Management** • Identify and treat the underlying cause • Monitor • Fluid intake/output • Blood pressure • Heart rate • Urine output • Serum electrolytes • Mild dehydration oral fluids or oral rehydration solution when appropriate • Severe dehydration IV fluid replacement usually with appropriate isotonic crystalloid depending on the clinical situation • Replace deficient electrolytes appropriately • Restrict fluids when clinically indicated for specific fluid overload conditions • Avoid rapid or excessive correction of electrolyte abnormalities • Monitor the patients clinical response **Physiotherapy Precaution** • Check the patients vital signs and medical stability before treatment • Modify or postpone intensive physiotherapy in significant dehydration or electrolyte disturbance • Encourage appropriate mobility and hydration according to the medical plan ///////////////////////////// **Drains and Tubes ICD Foley Catheter Hemovac CVC and PICC** --- **1 Drains Definition** • A surgical drain is a tube or device inserted into a body cavity wound or surgical site to remove blood pus serous fluid bile or other unwanted collections • Drains help prevent fluid accumulation and may reduce complications such as infection or hematoma --- **2 ICD Intercostal Drain** **Definition** • An intercostal drain ICD is a tube inserted through the chest wall into the pleural cavity to remove air blood or fluid and allow the lung to re expand **Indications** • Pneumothorax • Hemothorax • Pleural effusion • Empyema • Post thoracic surgery **Physiotherapy precautions** • Check the drain system before treatment • Keep the drainage bottle/system upright and below chest level • Avoid pulling kinking or compressing the tube • Encourage breathing exercises and early mobilization when medically stable • Monitor SpO2 respiratory rate and symptoms --- **3 Foley Catheter** **Definition** • A Foley catheter is an indwelling urinary catheter inserted through the urethra into the urinary bladder to continuously drain urine • A balloon near the tip keeps the catheter in place **Indications** • Urinary retention • Accurate urine output monitoring in selected critically ill patients • Perioperative bladder drainage • Certain urological conditions **Physiotherapy precautions** • Keep the drainage bag below bladder level • Avoid kinking or pulling the catheter • Secure the catheter during mobilization • Encourage early mobility when appropriate • Maintain infection control precautions --- **4 Hemovac** **Definition** • Hemovac is a closed suction drainage system used to remove blood and other fluid from a surgical wound **Uses** • Orthopaedic surgery • Joint replacement • Spinal surgery • Other operations where postoperative fluid collection is expected **Physiotherapy precautions** • Check the drain and suction reservoir before treatment • Keep the reservoir secured • Avoid pulling or compressing the tubing • Monitor the amount and appearance of drainage • Mobilize carefully without tension on the drain --- **5 CVC Central Venous Catheter** **Definition** • A central venous catheter CVC is a catheter whose tip lies in a large central vein commonly the superior vena cava for administration of medications fluids or other therapies **Indications** • Administration of irritating/vesicant medications • Long term IV therapy • Administration of parenteral nutrition • Hemodynamic monitoring in selected patients • Poor peripheral venous access **Physiotherapy precautions** • Ensure the catheter is secure before movement • Avoid excessive traction on the catheter • Monitor the insertion site for redness swelling or bleeding • Observe for signs of infection or catheter complications • Coordinate with nursing/medical staff during mobilization --- **6 PICC Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter** **Definition** • A PICC is a long flexible catheter inserted through a peripheral vein usually in the upper arm with its tip advanced into a central vein **Indications** • Long term IV antibiotics • Long term medication administration • Chemotherapy • Parenteral nutrition • Repeated blood sampling in selected patients **Physiotherapy precautions** • Keep the PICC secure • Avoid pulling or excessive pressure on the catheter • Follow activity restrictions provided by the medical team • Monitor the arm for • Swelling • Pain • Redness • Bleeding • Stop treatment and report suspected catheter complications //////////////////// **Abdominal and Pelvic Surgeries Appendectomy Herniorrhaphy Cholecystectomy Hysterectomy Colostomy and Ileostomy** Point wise format for Physiotherapy Surgery Exam --- **1 APPENDECTOMY** **Definition** • Appendectomy is the surgical removal of the vermiform appendix • It is most commonly performed for acute appendicitis **Indications** • Acute uncomplicated appendicitis • Complicated appendicitis • Gangrenous appendix • Perforated appendix • Appendicular abscess in selected cases • Generalized peritonitis due to perforation • Appendiceal neoplasm in selected cases **Procedure / Surgical Steps** **Laparoscopic appendectomy** 1 General anesthesia is administered 2 Patient is positioned supine 3 Abdomen is prepared and draped 4 Pneumoperitoneum is created using CO2 5 Laparoscopic ports are inserted 6 Cecum and appendix are identified 7 Appendix is mobilized 8 Mesoappendix and appendicular vessels are divided 9 Base of appendix is secured and divided 10 Appendix is removed usually using a retrieval bag 11 Operative site is inspected for bleeding or contamination 12 CO2 is released 13 Ports are removed and wounds are closed **Flow** Anesthesia Pneumoperitoneum Identify appendix Divide mesoappendix Divide appendix Remove appendix Check Close **Complications** • Wound infection • Intra abdominal abscess • Bleeding/hematoma • Postoperative pain • Ileus • Adhesive intestinal obstruction • Peritonitis/sepsis in complicated cases • Stump appendicitis **Physiotherapy** • Assess vital signs pain and respiratory status • Deep breathing exercises • Thoracic expansion exercises • Supported coughing • Ankle pump exercises • Early mobilization • Bed mobility and transfer training • Progressive walking • Gentle ROM exercises • Teach log rolling for bed mobility when appropriate • Protect the abdominal wound • Avoid heavy lifting/strenuous activity until medically cleared --- **2 HERNIORRHAPHY** **Definition** • Herniorrhaphy is the surgical repair of a hernia by reducing the herniated contents and repairing the abdominal wall defect • Modern repairs frequently use mesh reinforcement when appropriate **Indications** • Symptomatic hernia • Painful hernia • Enlarging hernia • Irreducible/incarcerated hernia • Strangulated hernia • Intestinal obstruction caused by hernia • Recurrent hernia • Risk of future complications in selected patients **Procedure / Surgical Steps** 1 Patient is assessed and prepared 2 Appropriate anesthesia is administered 3 Incision is made over the hernia 4 Hernia sac is identified 5 Sac is dissected from surrounding structures 6 Herniated bowel/omentum is inspected 7 Viable contents are reduced into the abdominal cavity 8 Non viable bowel may require resection 9 Hernia sac is reduced or excised 10 Fascial defect is repaired 11 Mesh may be placed for reinforcement 12 Hemostasis is achieved 13 Wound is closed in layers **Complications** • Hematoma • Seroma • Wound infection • Chronic postoperative pain • Urinary retention • Nerve injury • Injury to bowel bladder or blood vessels • Mesh infection • Recurrence of hernia **Physiotherapy** • Deep breathing exercises • Supported coughing • Early mobilization • Ankle pump exercises • Gentle ROM exercises • Progressive walking • Teach safe bed mobility • Gradual abdominal strengthening after adequate healing and medical clearance • Avoid excessive straining and heavy lifting during the healing period • Educate regarding posture and body mechanics --- **3 CHOLECYSTECTOMY** **Definition** • Cholecystectomy is the surgical removal of the gallbladder • It is commonly performed laparoscopically **Indications** • Symptomatic gallstones • Acute cholecystitis • Chronic cholecystitis • Gallstone related complications • Gallstone pancreatitis in appropriate cases • Selected gallbladder polyps or tumours • Gallbladder trauma in selected cases **Procedure / Surgical Steps** **Laparoscopic cholecystectomy** 1 General anesthesia is administered 2 Patient is positioned appropriately 3 Abdomen is prepared and draped 4 Pneumoperitoneum is created with CO2 5 Laparoscopic ports are inserted 6 Gallbladder is exposed and retracted 7 Hepatocystic/Calots triangle is exposed 8 Cystic duct and cystic artery are identified 9 Critical view of safety is obtained 10 Cystic duct and artery are clipped/ligated and divided 11 Gallbladder is dissected from the liver bed 12 Gallbladder is removed through a port 13 Hemostasis and bile leakage are checked 14 Pneumoperitoneum is released 15 Ports are removed and wounds are closed **Complications** • Bleeding • Wound infection • Bile leak • Bile duct injury • Injury to surrounding organs • Retained common bile duct stone • Postoperative nausea/vomiting • Conversion to open surgery when required **Physiotherapy** • Assess respiratory status • Deep breathing and thoracic expansion exercises • Supported coughing • Appropriate positioning • Ankle pump exercises • Early mobilization • Progressive walking • Gentle ROM • Wound protection • Gradual return to functional activities --- **4 HYSTERECTOMY** **Definition** • Hysterectomy is the surgical removal of the uterus • Depending on the procedure the cervix fallopian tubes and ovaries may also be removed **Indications** • Abnormal uterine bleeding • Uterine fibroids/leiomyoma • Gynecological malignancy • Premalignant disease • Endometriosis • Pelvic organ prolapse • Chronic pelvic pain in selected cases • Pelvic infection in selected cases **Types** • Total hysterectomy • Subtotal/supracervical hysterectomy • Radical hysterectomy • Hysterectomy with removal of tubes and ovaries when indicated **Procedure / Surgical Steps Abdominal Hysterectomy** 1 Patient is assessed and prepared 2 General anesthesia is administered 3 Patient is positioned appropriately 4 Abdomen is prepared and draped 5 Abdominal incision is made 6 Pelvic organs are inspected 7 Uterus is mobilized 8 Supporting ligaments are identified and divided 9 Uterine vessels are ligated and divided 10 Bladder is separated from the lower uterus/cervix 11 Uterus and cervix are removed 12 Vaginal cuff is closed 13 Hemostasis is checked 14 Abdominal wound is closed in layers **Complications** • Hemorrhage • Wound infection • UTI • Pelvic infection/abscess • DVT/VTE • Bladder injury • Ureteric injury • Bowel injury • Ileus • Nerve injury • Vaginal cuff dehiscence • Urinary dysfunction **Physiotherapy** • Assess pain respiratory status and mobility • Deep breathing exercises • Circulatory exercises • Early mobilization • Bed mobility and transfer training • Gentle ROM exercises • Progressive walking • Postural education • Pelvic floor muscle exercises when appropriate • Gradual abdominal strengthening • Avoid heavy lifting until medically cleared • Educate regarding safe return to ADLs --- **5 COLOSTOMY** **Definition** • Colostomy is a surgical procedure in which a portion of the colon is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma allowing feces to leave the body through the stoma • It may be temporary or permanent **Indications** • Colorectal cancer • Bowel obstruction • Bowel perforation • Diverticular disease with obstruction • Inflammatory bowel disease • Traumatic bowel injury • Protection of a distal bowel anastomosis • Gangrenous sigmoid volvulus • Severe perineal/anorectal disease • Anastomotic leak • Fecal diversion **Types** • End colostomy • Loop colostomy • Double barrel colostomy • Temporary or permanent colostomy **Procedure / Surgical Steps** 1 Patient is assessed and prepared 2 Stoma site is selected and marked 3 General anesthesia is administered 4 Abdomen is accessed by open or laparoscopic technique 5 Diseased bowel is identified 6 Bowel resection is performed if required 7 Appropriate colon segment is selected 8 Abdominal wall opening is created 9 Colon is brought through the opening without excessive tension 10 Colon is opened 11 Bowel edge is sutured to the skin 12 Stoma is matured 13 Abdominal wound is closed 14 Stoma appliance is applied **Complications** • Stoma ischemia/necrosis • Stoma retraction • Stoma prolapse • Stenosis • Peristomal skin irritation • Parastomal hernia • Bleeding • Hematoma • Bowel obstruction/ileus • Wound infection • DVT • Atelectasis/pneumonia **Physiotherapy** • Respiratory exercises • Deep breathing and supported coughing • Early mobilization • Ankle pump exercises • Bed mobility and transfer training • Progressive walking • Avoid direct pressure over the stoma • Protect the stoma appliance during movement • Gentle ROM exercises • Gradual strengthening • Avoid excessive abdominal pressure in the early healing period • Teach safe posture and body mechanics • Coordinate with the stoma care team --- **6 ILEOSTOMY** **Definition** • Ileostomy is a procedure in which the ileum is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma allowing intestinal contents to leave the body through the stoma • It can be temporary or permanent and may be an end or loop ileostomy **Indications** • Ulcerative colitis • Crohns disease • Colorectal cancer • Total colectomy/proctocolectomy • Severe bowel inflammation • Bowel perforation • Bowel ischemia • Protection of a distal intestinal/colorectal anastomosis • Anastomotic leak • Severe perineal disease **Types** • End ileostomy • Loop ileostomy • Temporary ileostomy • Permanent ileostomy **Procedure / Surgical Steps** 1 Patient is assessed and prepared 2 Appropriate stoma site is selected and marked 3 General anesthesia is administered 4 Abdomen is accessed 5 Diseased bowel is identified and resected when required 6 Suitable ileal segment is selected 7 Opening is created in the abdominal wall 8 Ileum is brought through the opening 9 Ileum is checked for adequate blood supply and absence of tension 10 Ileum is opened 11 Stoma is everted/spouted to direct intestinal contents away from the skin 12 Ileum is sutured to the skin 13 Abdominal wound is closed 14 Stoma appliance is applied **Complications** • Dehydration • Fluid and electrolyte imbalance • High output ileostomy • Stoma ischemia/necrosis • Stoma retraction • Stoma prolapse • Stenosis • Peristomal skin irritation • Parastomal hernia • Bleeding • Obstruction • Infection/abscess • Renal impairment secondary to significant fluid loss **Physiotherapy** • Assess general condition and hydration status • Respiratory exercises • Circulatory exercises • Early mobilization • Bed mobility and transfer training • Progressive walking • Gentle ROM exercises • Protect the stoma during transfers and exercise • Avoid excessive abdominal pressure during early healing • Progressive strengthening • Monitor fatigue and exercise tolerance • Educate regarding safe activity and stoma protection • Coordinate with the stoma care team ///// ///////////////////// **1 CT vs MRI** --- **CT Computed Tomography** • Uses X rays and computer processing to produce cross sectional images • Fast and widely available • Excellent for • Acute intracranial hemorrhage • Head trauma • Skull fractures • Bone abnormalities • Uses ionizing radiation • Provides less soft tissue contrast than MRI --- **MRI Magnetic Resonance Imaging** • Uses a strong magnetic field and radiofrequency waves • Does not use ionizing radiation • Provides excellent soft tissue contrast • Better for • Brain and spinal cord lesions • Tumours • Demyelinating disease • Early ischemic stroke • Ligament muscle and joint pathology • More time consuming and may be contraindicated or require special precautions with certain implants --- **2 Indications of CT and MRI** **Indications of CT** • Head injury and skull fracture • Suspected intracranial hemorrhage • Acute neurological emergencies • Stroke assessment • Bone pathology • Chest and abdominal emergencies • Detection of masses and structural abnormalities **Indications of MRI** • Brain tumours • Spinal cord and nerve lesions • Multiple sclerosis/demyelination • Early ischemic stroke • Joint and soft tissue injuries • Ligament and tendon pathology • Detailed assessment of brain and spinal lesions --- **3 X Ray Interpretation Basics** **A Check the patient and film** • Confirm patient identity • Check the correct body part and side • Identify the view/projection AP PA lateral etc • Check image quality and positioning **B Use a systematic approach** For a chest X ray a common approach is ABCDE • A Airway • Tracheal position • Airway patency • B Breathing • Lung fields • Pneumothorax • Consolidation • Pleural effusion • C Circulation • Heart size and cardiac silhouette • Mediastinum • D Disability/diaphragm • Diaphragms • Costophrenic angles • Check for free air where relevant • E Everything else • Bones • Soft tissues • Lines tubes and medical devices **Important X ray findings** • Fracture break/discontinuity in bone cortex • Dislocation abnormal relationship of joint surfaces • Consolidation increased lung opacity often associated with infection • Pneumothorax pleural air with absent peripheral lung markings • Pleural effusion fluid causing blunting of the costophrenic angle /////////////////////// **PAOD Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease** --- **1 Definition** • Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease PAOD is a condition in which arterial blood flow to the peripheral limbs is reduced due to narrowing or blockage of arteries most commonly due to atherosclerosis nhs uk --- **2 Causes / Risk Factors** • Atherosclerosis • Smoking • Diabetes mellitus • Hypertension • Hyperlipidemia • Increasing age • Obesity • Family history of cardiovascular disease --- **3 Clinical Features** • Intermittent claudication pain/cramping in the limb during walking that improves with rest • Cold extremity • Pale or bluish skin • Reduced/absent peripheral pulses • Shiny thin skin • Loss of limb hair • Delayed wound healing • Ischemic ulcers • Severe disease rest pain and gangrene --- **4 Complications** • Chronic ischemia • Non healing ulcers • Tissue necrosis • Gangrene • Limb loss/amputation • Increased cardiovascular risk including heart attack and stroke nhs uk --- **5 Management** • Smoking cessation • Supervised/structured exercise therapy • Control of diabetes blood pressure and cholesterol • Antiplatelet and lipid lowering medication as prescribed • Angioplasty/stenting for selected patients • Arterial bypass surgery for selected severe disease • Amputation may be required for irreversible tissue loss **Physiotherapy Role** • Graded walking/exercise programme as appropriate • Lower limb strengthening • Functional mobility training • Foot and skin care education • Monitor pain skin condition and peripheral circulation /////////////////// **Surgical Infections Classification** --- **Definition** • Surgical infection is an infection involving a wound tissue or organ that occurs before during or after a surgical procedure and may interfere with normal healing --- **Classification of Surgical Infections** **1 According to Clinical Course** • Acute surgical infections • Develop rapidly • Usually associated with acute inflammation • Examples • Cellulitis • Abscess • Acute wound infection • Necrotizing soft tissue infection • Chronic surgical infections • Persist for a prolonged period • May cause fibrosis sinus formation or tissue destruction • Examples • Tuberculosis • Chronic osteomyelitis • Chronic wound infection --- **2 According to Site** • Superficial infections • Involve skin and subcutaneous tissue • Examples cellulitis superficial wound infection • Deep infections • Involve deep fascia muscle or deeper tissues • Examples deep incisional surgical site infection deep abscess • Organ/space infections • Involve an organ or body space related to surgery • Examples • Intra abdominal abscess • Pelvic abscess • Postoperative pneumonia --- **3 According to Source** • Exogenous infection • Organisms enter from outside the patients body • Example contamination from instruments environment or healthcare personnel • Endogenous infection • Caused by microorganisms already present in the patients own body • Example infection caused by intestinal flora following bowel surgery --- **4 Surgical Site Infection SSI Classification** • Superficial incisional SSI • Involves skin and subcutaneous tissue • Deep incisional SSI • Involves deep soft tissues such as fascia and muscle • Organ/space SSI • Involves any part of the body deeper than the fascia/muscle that was opened or manipulated during surgery cdc gov --- **5 According to Presence of Pus** • Non suppurative infection • Does not primarily produce pus • Example cellulitis • Suppurative infection • Characterized by pus formation • Examples • Abscess • Empyema • 