{"step_index":7,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T13:56:53Z","content":"**Anesthesia** **Types General Spinal and Local Systemic Effects** **1 Definition** • Anesthesia is a medically induced temporary and usually reversible state in which sensation especially pain is reduced or abolished with or without loss of consciousness to permit surgical or diagnostic procedures --- **2 Types of Anesthesia** **A General Anesthesia** **Definition** • General anesthesia is a drug induced state of unconsciousness with loss of sensation and reduced protective reflexes during surgery **Features** • Patient is unconscious • Pain sensation is suppressed • Protective airway reflexes are reduced • Muscle relaxation may be produced • Airway support/ventilation may be required **Uses** • Major abdominal surgery • Thoracic surgery • Neurosurgery • Major orthopaedic surgery • Procedures that require prolonged immobility --- **B Spinal Anesthesia** **Definition** • Spinal anesthesia is a regional anesthetic technique in which a local anesthetic is injected into the subarachnoid/intrathecal space producing temporary sensory and motor blockade **Features** • Patient is usually conscious • Produces numbness and weakness below the level of the block • Commonly used for lower abdominal pelvic and lower limb surgery **Common effects/complications** • Hypotension • Bradycardia • Urinary retention • Post dural puncture headache • Temporary motor weakness • Rare neurological complications --- **C Local Anesthesia** **Definition** • Local anesthesia produces temporary loss of sensation in a specific limited area without causing loss of consciousness **Examples** • Local infiltration for wound suturing • Dental anesthesia • Minor skin procedures • Peripheral nerve blocks **Mechanism** • Local anesthetics block voltage gated sodium channels preventing nerve impulse transmission --- **3 Systemic Effects of Anesthesia** **A Respiratory System** • Respiratory depression particularly with general anesthesia and sedative/opioid drugs • Reduced cough and airway protective reflexes • Hypoventilation • Reduced functional respiratory capacity • Risk of atelectasis and postoperative pulmonary complications **B Cardiovascular System** • Hypotension • Bradycardia or tachycardia • Reduced vascular tone • Changes in cardiac output • Significant spinal blockade may produce hypotension and bradycardia **C Central Nervous System** • Loss of consciousness with general anesthesia • Reduced awareness and reflexes • Drowsiness and dizziness after anesthesia • Temporary confusion or memory impairment may occur particularly in susceptible patients **D Gastrointestinal System** • Nausea and vomiting • Reduced gastrointestinal motility • Postoperative ileus may occur after major surgery **E Urinary System** • Urinary retention particularly following neuraxial anesthesia and some surgical procedures **F Musculoskeletal System** • Muscle relaxation during general anesthesia • Temporary weakness after regional/spinal anesthesia • Reduced mobility following surgery • Increased risk of deconditioning with prolonged immobilization **G Temperature Regulation** • Hypothermia may occur during and after surgery • Shivering may occur particularly after spinal anesthesia --- **4 Physiotherapy Implications** • Assess HR BP RR and SpO2 before treatment • Assess level of consciousness and neurological status • Check motor and sensory recovery after spinal anesthesia • Begin mobilization only when the patient is medically stable and adequate motor/sensory function has returned • Perform deep breathing exercises when indicated • Encourage ankle pump and limb exercises • Progress from bed mobility sitting standing walking • Monitor for • Dizziness • Hypotension • Desaturation • Excessive weakness • Altered consciousness • Use appropriate positioning and airway clearance techniques when clinically indicated ////////////////////// --- **1 CELLULITIS** **Definition** • Cellulitis is an acute bacterial infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissues **Causes / Risk Factors** • Bacterial entry through cuts wounds or abrasions • Insect bites • Fungal skin infection • Diabetes mellitus • Lymphoedema • Poor circulation • Immunosuppression **Clinical Features** • Redness • Warmth • Swelling • Pain and tenderness • Fever and chills • Malaise • Regional lymph node swelling **Complications** • Abscess formation • Lymphangitis • Osteomyelitis • Sepsis • Necrotizing soft tissue infection • Recurrent cellulitis **Diagnosis** • Clinical examination • Blood tests if systemic infection is suspected • Wound culture if there is discharge • Imaging if deep infection is suspected **Treatment** • Appropriate antibiotics • Analgesics • Limb elevation when appropriate • Treatment of underlying conditions • IV antibiotics/hospitalization for severe infection **Physiotherapy Implications** • Assess pain swelling and mobility • Avoid vigorous massage over acute cellulitis • Elevate the affected limb when appropriate • Maintain mobility of unaffected areas • Gradually resume exercises after infection improves • Educate regarding skin care and prevention --- **2 ABSCESS 10 Marks** **Definition** • An abscess is a localized collection of pus within a tissue or body cavity usually caused by infection **Causes** • Bacterial infection • Infected wound • Blocked skin glands/hair follicles • Infected cyst • Foreign body • Diabetes and reduced immunity **Types** • Cutaneous abscess • Subcutaneous abscess • Deep abscess • Intra abdominal abscess • Pelvic abscess • Liver abscess • Lung abscess **Clinical Features** • Painful swelling • Redness • Warmth • Tenderness • Fluctuant swelling • Pus discharge • Fever • Malaise **Complications** • Cellulitis • Spread of infection • Sepsis • Tissue destruction • Recurrence • Fistula formation **Diagnosis** • Clinical examination • Ultrasound for selected collections • CT/MRI for deep abscesses • Culture of pus when appropriate **Treatment** • Incision and drainage is the main treatment for many abscesses • Antibiotics when clinically indicated • Analgesics • Wound dressing • Treatment of underlying disease **Physiotherapy Implications** • Avoid direct pressure over acute abscess • Avoid vigorous massage over infected tissue • Maintain mobility of unaffected areas • Provide postoperative respiratory and mobility exercises when indicated • Monitor for systemic infection • Follow wound care precautions --- **3 GANGRENE 10 Marks** **Definition** • Gangrene is the death of body tissue usually due to loss of blood supply infection or both **Causes** • Peripheral arterial disease • Diabetes mellitus • Severe infection • Trauma • Atherosclerosis • Arterial obstruction • Burns or frostbite **Types** • Dry gangrene Usually due to severe ischemia • Wet gangrene Necrotic tissue associated with infection • Gas gangrene Severe bacterial infection with gas production in tissues • Internal gangrene Affects internal organs following loss of blood supply **Clinical Features** • Black brown purple or discoloured tissue • Coldness • Loss of sensation • Pain • Swelling • Blisters or ulcers • Foul smelling discharge in infected gangrene • Fever and systemic illness **Complications** • Severe infection • Sepsis • Shock • Extensive tissue loss • Amputation • Death if untreated **Treatment** • Restore blood supply when possible • Surgical debridement • Antibiotics for infection • Vascular surgery when indicated • Amputation for irreversible tissue damage **Physiotherapy Implications** • Assess circulation and functional status • Avoid pressure or trauma to ischemic tissue • Maintain ROM of unaffected joints • After amputation • Stump care • ROM exercises • Strengthening • Balance training • Prosthetic rehabilitation --- **4 TUBERCULOSIS TB 10 Marks** **Definition** • Tuberculosis TB is an infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis most commonly affecting the lungs **Types** • Pulmonary TB Affects the lungs • Extrapulmonary TB Affects organs outside the lungs such as • Lymph nodes • Spine • Bones and joints • Kidneys • Brain/meninges • Skin **Risk Factors** • Close contact with infectious TB • HIV infection • Diabetes mellitus • Malnutrition • Smoking • Immunosuppression • Overcrowded living conditions **Clinical Features** • Persistent cough • Sputum production • Coughing blood may occur • Chest pain • Fever • Night sweats • Weight loss • Loss of appetite • Fatigue **Diagnosis** • Sputum examination • Rapid molecular diagnostic tests • Chest X ray • Culture and drug susceptibility testing • TST or IGRA for TB infection **Treatment** • Combination anti tuberculosis drugs • Common first line drugs • Rifampicin • Isoniazid • Pyrazinamide • Ethambutol • Complete the prescribed treatment course to prevent treatment failure and drug resistance **Complications** • Pulmonary fibrosis • Bronchiectasis • Hemoptysis • Respiratory impairment • Pleural disease • TB meningitis • Spinal TB • Bone and joint destruction • Disseminated/miliary TB **Physiotherapy Implications** • Assess respiratory status and exercise tolerance • Monitor HR RR and SpO2 • Breathing exercises when indicated • Gradual aerobic and functional exercise • Postural and chest mobility exercises • Energy conservation techniques • Follow airborne infection control precautions for infectious pulmonary TB //////////////// --- **Hospital Acquired Infections HAI** **1 Definition** • Hospital acquired infection HAI also called healthcare associated infection is an infection that a patient acquires while receiving healthcare or during/after hospitalization which was not present or incubating at the time of admission • HAIs are often preventable and are an important cause of increased morbidity mortality and hospital stay --- **2 Common Types of Hospital Acquired Infections** • Catheter associated urinary tract infection CAUTI • Associated with urinary catheterization • Surgical site infection SSI • Infection occurring at or near the surgical site after an operation • Central line associated bloodstream infection CLABSI • Bloodstream infection associated with a central venous catheter • Hospital acquired pneumonia • Pneumonia developing during healthcare/hospitalization • Clostridioides difficile infection • May occur following disruption of normal intestinal flora particularly after antibiotic exposure • Bloodstream infections • May occur from vascular access devices or other sources --- **3 Causative Organisms** • Bacteria • Staphylococcus aureus • Escherichia coli • Klebsiella species • Pseudomonas aeruginosa • Enterococcus species • Viruses • Influenza and other respiratory viruses • Fungi • Candida species • Some healthcare associated pathogens may be antimicrobial resistant making treatment more difficult --- **4 Risk Factors** **Patient related factors** • Extremes of age • Poor nutritional status • Diabetes mellitus • Immunosuppression • Severe underlying illness • Reduced mobility • Prolonged hospitalization **Treatment/procedure related factors** • Surgery • Urinary catheterization • Central venous catheterization • Mechanical ventilation • Prolonged antibiotic use • Invasive procedures • Poor wound care --- **5 Modes of Transmission** • Contact transmission • Direct contact between people • Indirect transmission through contaminated hands or equipment • Droplet transmission • Respiratory droplets from coughing sneezing or talking • Airborne transmission • Small infectious particles remaining suspended in air important for diseases such as pulmonary tuberculosis • Common vehicle transmission • Contaminated food water medications or equipment • Self infection/endogenous transmission • Organisms from the patients own flora cause infection especially when normal barriers are disrupted --- **6 Clinical Manifestations** Depending on the site of infection • Fever • Chills • Increased heart rate • Increased respiratory rate • Local redness warmth and swelling • Pain or tenderness • Purulent wound discharge • Cough and sputum in respiratory infection • Dysuria and urinary frequency in UTI • Altered mental status in severe infection • Sepsis in severe cases --- **7 Prevention and Control** • Strict hand hygiene before and after patient contact • Use appropriate personal protective equipment • Maintain aseptic technique • Proper sterilization and disinfection of instruments • Safe injection practices • Appropriate wound care • Proper catheter care • Remove urinary and vascular catheters as soon as they are no longer required • Appropriate isolation precautions • Environmental cleaning • Appropriate antibiotic use and antimicrobial stewardship • Surveillance and early identification of infections --- **8 Physiotherapy Implications** • Follow standard infection control precautions during treatment • Perform hand hygiene before and after patient contact • Use appropriate PPE according to the infection and isolation precautions • Assess • Temperature • HR • RR • SpO2 • General condition //////////////// --- **Stereotactic Surgery DBS Rhizotomy Thalamotomy Neural Implants** **1 Stereotactic Surgery** **Definition** • Stereotactic surgery is a minimally invasive neurosurgical technique that uses a three dimensional coordinate system with CT/MRI guidance to accurately locate and treat a specific area of the brain **Overview** • Uses precise anatomical targeting • May use a stereotactic frame or frameless navigation • Can be used for • Deep brain stimulation • Brain biopsy • Lesioning procedures • Stereotactic radiosurgery • Treatment of selected brain tumours and vascular lesions **Indications** • Brain tumours • Arteriovenous malformations • Movement disorders • Parkinsons disease • Essential tremor • Dystonia • Epilepsy • Selected functional neurological disorders **Complications** • Intracranial bleeding • Infection • Seizures • Neurological deficit • Brain edema • Headache • Injury to surrounding brain structures • Radiation related complications when radiosurgery is used --- **2 Deep Brain Stimulation DBS** **Definition** • Deep Brain Stimulation is a treatment in which electrodes are implanted into specific areas of the brain to deliver controlled electrical impulses with a pulse generator usually implanted under the skin of the chest **Overview** • DBS is a reversible neuromodulation technique • Common targets include • Subthalamic nucleus STN • Globus pallidus internus GPi • Ventral intermediate nucleus VIM of the thalamus • The electrodes are connected by wires to an implanted pulse generator **Indications** • Parkinsons disease • Essential tremor • Dystonia • Treatment resistant obsessive compulsive disorder in selected patients • Selected refractory epilepsy • Other indications may be considered in specialist centres **Complications** • Intracranial hemorrhage • Stroke • Infection • Seizures • Lead displacement or malfunction • Hardware failure • Headache • Confusion • Speech or balance problems • Tingling or muscle contractions due to stimulation • Mood changes --- **3 Rhizotomy** **Definition** • Rhizotomy is a surgical procedure in which selected nerve roots or their sensory fibres are partially or completely divided/ablated to reduce abnormal nerve activity or pain **Overview** • It may be performed using surgical radiofrequency or other lesioning techniques depending on the nerve and condition • The aim is to interrupt abnormal sensory or motor pathways **Indications** • Severe chronic pain in selected patients • Trigeminal neuralgia in selected cases • Spasticity in selected patients particularly through selective dorsal rhizotomy • Certain refractory neurological conditions **Complications** • Numbness or loss of sensation • Weakness • Neuropathic pain • Infection • Bleeding • Unintended nerve injury • Recurrence of symptoms **Physiotherapy relevance** • Assess sensory and motor function • Manage weakness and altered sensation • Improve balance and gait when required • Stretching and strengthening for patients treated for spasticity --- **4 Thalamotomy** **Definition** • Thalamotomy is a surgical procedure in which a small precisely targeted area of the thalamus is destroyed or lesioned to reduce abnormal neurological activity **Overview** • It is usually performed using stereotactic guidance • Modern techniques may include radiofrequency lesioning or focused ultrasound • VIM thalamotomy targets the ventral intermediate nucleus and is primarily used for tremor **Indications** • Severe essential tremor • Selected tremor dominant Parkinsons disease • Medication resistant tremor when appropriate • Selected unilateral movement disorders **Complications** • Speech disturbance/dysarthria • Ataxia • Balance and gait disturbance • Weakness • Sensory disturbance • Cognitive or neurological deficits depending on the target • Hemorrhage or infection with invasive procedures --- **5 Neural Implants** **Definition** • Neural implants are medical devices implanted in or near the nervous system to record neural activity or deliver electrical stimulation to modify nervous system function **Examples** • Deep brain stimulators • Vagus nerve stimulators • Responsive neurostimulation systems • Spinal cord stimulators • Cochlear implants **Indications** • Parkinsons disease • Essential tremor • Epilepsy • Chronic neuropathic pain • Hearing loss • Selected movement disorders • Selected psychiatric or neurological conditions **Complications** • Infection • Bleeding • Device malfunction • Lead migration or breakage • Pain at the implantation site • Nerve or brain injury • Stimulation related adverse effects //////////// --- **Radiological Interpretation of OA and RA** **1 Osteoarthritis OA** • OA is a degenerative joint disease characterized by progressive cartilage loss and changes in the subchondral bone • X ray findings • Non uniform joint space narrowing • Osteophytes marginal bony outgrowths • Subchondral sclerosis • Subchondral cysts • Bony remodeling/deformity in advanced disease • Usually affects weight bearing joints such as the knee and hip • Often has an asymmetrical distribution **2 Rheumatoid Arthritis RA** • RA is a chronic autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting the synovial joints • X ray findings • Symmetrical joint space narrowing • Marginal bone erosions • Periarticular osteopenia • Soft tissue swelling • Subluxation and deformity in advanced disease • Commonly affects MCP PIP and wrist joints DIP joints are usually spared //// /////////","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right one is paramount. My approach is to always prioritize the appropriate tool for each specific task, and avoid unnecessary complexity. The goal is to be efficient and precise in my actions.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in refining my tool selection strategy. Remembering the critical instructions, I am committed to always using the most specialized tool possible. I've begun to explicitly list all potentially relevant tools before making a call. My focus is now on eliminating unnecessary `cat`, `grep`, `ls` and `sed` calls, aiming for a more streamlined approach.\n\n\n**Analyzing Anesthesia**\n\nI'm now analyzing anesthesia 1 I am evaluating different anesthesia types 2 assessing risks and 3 benefits I will use this to determine the optimal approach for this specific scenario\n\n\n**Evaluating Anesthesia Details**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the specifics of anesthesia. I've classified types as general, spinal, and local, acknowledging systemic effects. I'm focusing on definitions and the different categories, including their characteristics. I'm extracting and structuring the key features, avoiding excessive punctuation to enhance clarity.\n\n\n**Defining Anesthesia Types**\n\nI'm now diving into the specifics of General and Spinal Anesthesia. I've focused on definitions, extracting key features like loss of sensation 